        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Martin Heidegger, who began his philosophical career in a context deeply shaped by Neo-Kantianism and wrote an early habilitation thesis on a text then attributed to Duns Scotus, came to regard the entire Neo-Kantian project as a symptom of philosophy\'s long forgetting of its most fundamental question: the question of Being. For Heidegger, Cohen\'s system — however internally sophisticated — was still operating entirely within the framework of epistemology, asking how knowledge is valid rather than asking what it means for anything to be at all. Cohen\'s Ursprung, the self-generating logical act of pure thought, was for Heidegger just another version of the modern metaphysical reduction of Being to the activity of a subject. In making thought the producer of reality, Cohen did not escape metaphysics — he perfected it in its most subjectivist form.' },
        { type: 'paragraph', text: 'In Being and Time (1927), Heidegger proposed a radically different starting point: not consciousness, not logical thought, not the transcendental subject, but Dasein — the concrete, thrown, mortal, care-structured being that we each are. Dasein does not produce reality through logical acts; it finds itself always already in a world it did not choose, in a historical situation that came before it, pressed upon by possibilities it did not generate. The category of thrownness — Geworfenheit — was Heidegger\'s pointed rejoinder to the Neo-Kantian fantasy of self-grounding thought. We are not the origins of our world; we are always late arrivals, finding ourselves in medias res, inheritors of a tradition and a mortality we cannot think our way out of.' }
