    bio: 'Paul Natorp was born on January 24, 1854, in Düsseldorf, Germany, and became one of the central figures of the Marburg School of Neo-Kantianism alongside his colleague and friend Hermann Cohen. He studied classical philology and philosophy at the universities of Berlin, Bonn, and Strasbourg before securing a professorship at the University of Marburg in 1885, where he would remain for the rest of his academic career. Quiet, exacting, and deeply learned, Natorp was in many ways the systematic complement to Cohen\'s more visionary philosophical style: where Cohen moved by bold speculative leaps, Natorp worked through the implications of Neo-Kantian idealism with patient, architectural precision.\n\nNatorp\'s philosophical interests ranged from the foundations of mathematics and natural science to psychology, education, and social philosophy. His Plato\'s Theory of Ideas (1903) was a landmark reinterpretation of Plato that argued the Forms were not metaphysical entities in a separate realm but methodological ideals — the regulative horizons toward which scientific and ethical inquiry perpetually strive. His work on psychology, particularly his Allgemeine Psychologie (1912), attempted to reconstruct psychological concepts on a purely methodological basis, arguing that the psychological subject could not be treated as a given empirical object but only as a logical construction generated through the systematic methods of psychological science. Later in his career, Natorp moved toward a more speculative, almost mystical philosophy influenced by German Idealism and the thought of Meister Eckhart.\n\nNatorp was also a committed social philosopher and educator, deeply engaged with the German labor movement and the cause of social democracy. His Sozialpädagogik (Social Pedagogy, 1899) argued that the development of individual human beings as moral and rational agents was inseparable from the cultivation of genuine community, and that education was therefore a fundamentally social and political enterprise. He corresponded with leading socialist thinkers and sought to connect the abstract principles of Neo-Kantian ethics to the concrete struggles of working people. Among his students were figures who would go on to reshape twentieth-century philosophy, including Hans-Georg Gadamer, who acknowledged Natorp as a profound early influence on his own hermeneutical thinking.',
